id":"four-equal-areas","label":"Use four equal areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"four-equal-areas":["cnm_186","cnm_113","cnm_p089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_113","cnm_p089","cnm_055"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_112","cnm_064"],"id":"midsegment-height","label":"Find the triangle's height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midsegment-height":["cnm_186","cnm_185","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_185","cnm_p148","cnm_055","cnm_112","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 5","reason":"The circle has 12 integer points: four axial points and eight points with coordinate magnitudes 3 and 4. Removing the two fixed vertices leaves 10 points, forming five antipodal pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1995_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_h173","cnm_250"],"id":"diagonal-circle","label":"Count possible diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonal-circle":["math:lattice-counting","cnm_186","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["math:lattice-counting","cnm_186","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_082","cnm_h173","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, BE = 2 - sqrt(3)","reason":"Let w=BE, a=JA and b=AG. Corner-triangle similarity gives EK=wa and EJ=wb, hence b+wa=1 and a+w(1+b)=1. Also a²+b²=1. Since a>0, a²=(1-b)(1+b)=wa(1+b) gives a=w(1+b), so a=1/2. Thus b=sqrt(3)/2 and w=(1-b)/a=2-sqrt(3).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p077","cnm_152"],"id":"solution:right-triangle-similarity:94370c91f551","label":"Similar right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-triangle-similarity":["cnm_120","cnm_186","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_120","cnm_186","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174","cnm_055","cnm_p077","cnm_152"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 67; possible areas are 9/5 and 5/3","reason":"The outer side is √10. After multiplying coordinates by √10 and placing the origin at the upper-left corner, the shared vertex of R is (4,3), and the long rectangle edge is 3y=x+11. Writing R's top vertex as (h,0), the rectangle conditions give (h-7)(h-8)=0. These yield actual areas 9/5 and 5/3, whose sum is 52/15. Thus m+n=67.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_10B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_174","cnm_177","cnm_183","cnm_p173","cnm_p168"],"id":"coordinates-and-pythagoras","label":"Coordinates and perpendicular sides","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinates-and-pythagoras":["cnm_084","cnm_245","cnm_186","cnm_188","cnm_224","cnm_p089","math:similar-figure-area-scaling"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_245","cnm_186","cnm_188","cnm_224","cnm_p089","math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_p077","cnm_174","cnm_177","cnm_183","cnm_p173","cnm_p168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 8r^2","reason":"Take A=(0,0), B=(w,0), D=(0,2r). The circle has center (r,r), and the diagonal midpoint is (w/2,r). Its membership on the circle gives (w/2-r)^2=r^2. Nondegeneracy excludes w=0, so w=4r and the area is 8r^2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_260","cnm_p188","cnm_p089"],"id":"midsegment-diameter","label":"Find a diameter through the diagonal midpoint","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midsegment-diameter":["cnm_262","cnm_186","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_186","cnm_185","cnm_260","cnm_p188","cnm_p089"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. Perpendicular","reason":"For example, BC is perpendicular to BA and BF because the adjoining faces are rectangles. Since BA and BF intersect in base plane ABFE, BC is perpendicular to that plane.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h120_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_186"],"id":"m1","label":"Two intersecting base edges","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h120"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h120","cnm_186"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, bx(h-x)/h","reason":"The small triangle above the rectangle has altitude h-x and is similar to the original triangle. Thus the rectangle's width is b(h-x)/h, and multiplying by its height x gives bx(h-x)/h.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q37","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_186"],"id":"similar-triangle-width","label":"Find the width by similarity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-triangle-width":["cnm_272","cnm_p089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_272","cnm_p089","cnm_271","cnm_186"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 6","reason":"Equal tangents give PT''=4 and QT''=9, so PQ=13. Since the parallel tangents touch opposite ends of a diameter, P and Q have vertical separation 2r and horizontal separation 9-4=5. Hence (2r)²+5²=13², giving r=6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1974_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-distance-triangle","label":"Distance between tangent endpoints","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-distance-triangle":["cnm_174","cnm_186"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_186"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_075"],"id":"tangent-lengths-and-pythagoras","label":"Tangent lengths and a right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-lengths-and-pythagoras":["cnm_262","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_186"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_186","cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 13 feet","reason":"During the required change of orientation, a table diagonal must become parallel to a wall, requiring S ≥ sqrt(10^2 + 8^2) = sqrt(164) ≈ 12.806. Centering the table allows rotation inside its circumcircle of that diameter. Thus 13 is feasible and 12 is not.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"diagonal-clearance","label":"Geometric diagonal clearance","nodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_176","cnm_245"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_244","cnm_072","cnm_079","cnm_250"],"id":"solution:diagonal-clearance:72c627528f43","label":"Diagonal clearance and a centered rotation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonal-clearance":["cnm_267","cnm_176","cnm_186","cnm_245"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_267","cnm_176","cnm_186","cnm_245","cnm_174","cnm_244","cnm_072","cnm_079","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 4.0","reason":"The perpendicular projections of A and B leave equal overhangs of (10-4)/2=3. Thus B's foot is 7 units from D. Since B is the midpoint of DE, its horizontal coordinate is half that of E, giving DF=14 and CF=14-10=4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1989_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"congruence-and-midsegments","label":"Projection congruence and midpoint theorem","methodDirectNodeIds":{"congruence-and-midsegments":["cnm_125","cnm_186","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_186","cnm_185"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (m/n)","reason":"For rectangle dimensions w and h, area(A) = (1/2)(h/n)(w/2) = wh/(4n), while area(B) = wh/(4m). Their ratio is m/n.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1996_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_p185"],"id":"base_height_ratio","label":"Compare the base-height products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"base_height_ratio":["cnm_p148","cnm_160"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_160","cnm_186","cnm_p185"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3","reason":"Squares and nonsquare rectangles have equidistant diagonal centers. An isosceles trapezoid's symmetry axis meets a leg's perpendicular bisector in an equidistant center. A cyclic parallelogram must have equal and supplementary opposite angles, hence be a rectangle, excluding the other two types. Thus 3 types qualify.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10A_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cyclic-quadrilateral-test","label":"Check for a circle through the vertices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cyclic-quadrilateral-test":["cnm_255","cnm_183","cnm_186","cnm_069"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_255","cnm_183","cnm_186","cnm_069"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (15)","reason":"Equal areas give XM = 2ZA, hence MY = 2YA. Since triangles WXM and MYA are similar, WX/XM = MY/YA = 2. Thus XM = 2, ZA = 1, YA = 3, and MY = 6. The desired area is 32 - 4 - 4 - (6·3/2) = 15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_116","cnm_270","cnm_p089"],"id":"similar-right-triangles","label":"Method 1: Similar right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-right-triangles":["cnm_p148","cnm_p150","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},